Ladybird now places a clock tick’s recording window, and presents the
tick’s frame, with the scroll offsets of the updated visual context
tree, whether or not the tick changed the tree’s structure. That’s a
matter of Ladybird’s own render-thread pipeline, which records a frame
off the main thread while a transition runs. So there’s no behavior of
Blink, WebKit or Gecko to compare it with: Their frames show a
scrolled pane’s content wherever it’s scrolled to, as ours now do.
